  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Safe Migration of Seasonal Workers from Central Asia to the UK</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The proposed project aims to support the safe participation of Central Asian migrants in the UK Seasonal Workers Scheme (SWS), promoting a positive migration experience within the United Kingdom (UK) and helping participating Central Asian countries to maximise its benefits to economic development. It aims to address the challenges faced by Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Uzbekistan nationals engaged in labour migration to the United Kingdom under the SWS. Historically, Central Asia has had a high dependence on the Russian Federation (RF) as the primary migrant-receiving country for Central Asian migrants; the UK has emerged as a new and popular destination country for Central Asians amid reduced migration to RF in the wake of the conflict in Ukraine. Furthermore, the combination of limited awareness among migrants regarding their rights and the UK SWS, coupled with the absence of a centralized source of reliable information, creates fertile ground for scams and misinformation to prey on the general public&#x2019;s lack of knowledge. This underscores the urgent need for intervention.Through multi-phase research and beneficiary feedback, it&apos;s evident that, although steps have been taken forward, migrants still lack essential knowledge and support systems for safe migration. While government agencies are improving their capacity to assist seasonal workers, turnover rates in Central Asian Government agencies and ad-hoc dismissals of trained staff remain high based on IOM observations in Phase I and II. Additionally, ongoing recruitments of new migrant cohorts highlight the need for continued support and information dissemination on the UK SWS.The project seeks to empower Central Asian migrants by enhancing their awareness and access to resources for safe migration to and from the UK. Interventions include providing free information, protection tools, and targeted orientation sessions and raising awareness on gender-based violence, prevention of sexual exploitation and abuse, and gender-related issues under the UK SWS. These efforts aim to ensure safer working conditions and improve migrants&apos; integration into the UK labour market.In the long term, the project aims to promote sustainable community development by empowering returnees and their families. Stakeholders engaged in the UK SWS will gain a deeper understanding of migrants&apos; needs, enabling proactive risk mitigation. By promoting informed migration and fostering a supportive environment, the project seeks to enhance migrants&apos; safety and well-being while contributing to broader socio-economic development within impacted communities.</narrative>
  </description>
